__d(function(a,e,n,t,o,i,s){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science \u2014 Geography",textbook:"The Earth: Our Habitat \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:18,chapter_title:"Major Landforms of the Earth",quiz_type:"MCQ only",mcqs:[{id:"mcq01",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is NOT one of the three major types of landforms?",options:["A) Mountain","B) Ocean","C) Plateau","D) Plain"],correct_answer:"B) Ocean",explanation:"The three major types of landforms are Mountains, Plateaus, and Plains. Oceans are part of the Hydrosphere, not a landform. Landforms refer to natural features of the solid land surface (Lithosphere)."},{id:"mcq02",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which is the highest mountain in the world?",options:["A) Mount K2","B) Mount Kilimanjaro","C) Mount Everest","D) Mount Fuji"],correct_answer:"C) Mount Everest",explanation:"Mount Everest (8,848.86 m) in the Himalayas, on the Nepal-China border, is the world's highest mountain. It was first climbed by Sir Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay Sherpa on 29 May 1953. It is known as 'Sagarmatha' in Nepal and 'Chomolungma' in Tibet."},{id:"mcq03",difficulty:"Easy",question:"A plateau is best described as:",options:["A) A flat low-lying area of land","B) An elevated flatland with steep sides","C) A mountain with a pointed peak","D) A deep valley between two mountains"],correct_answer:"B) An elevated flatland with steep sides",explanation:"A plateau is an elevated, flat-topped landform that rises steeply from the surrounding area on at least one side. It is often called a 'tableland' because of its table-like shape \u2014 flat top with steep sides. The Deccan Plateau is one of India's major plateaus."},{id:"mcq04",difficulty:"Easy",question:"The Himalayas are an example of which type of mountains?",options:["A) Volcanic mountains","B) Block mountains","C) Fold mountains","D) Residual mountains"],correct_answer:"C) Fold mountains",explanation:"The Himalayas are fold mountains, formed when the Indian Plate collided with the Eurasian Plate. The collision caused the rocks to fold and push upward, creating the Himalayas. Fold mountains are the most common type and are usually the highest and most extensive mountain ranges."},{id:"mcq05",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Plains are preferred for human settlement mainly because they have:",options:["A) Cold climate and thick forests","B) Flat land with fertile soil suitable for farming","C) High altitude that protects from floods","D) Dense mineral resources underground"],correct_answer:"B) Flat land with fertile soil suitable for farming",explanation:"Plains are preferred for human settlement because they offer flat land for farming, fertile soil (often alluvial), easy transport links, river water for irrigation, and mild climates. The Indo-Gangetic Plain is one of the most densely populated plains in the world."},{id:"mcq06",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is an example of a volcanic mountain?",options:["A) Mount Everest","B) Aravalli Hills","C) Vindhya Range","D) Mount Kilimanjaro"],correct_answer:"D) Mount Kilimanjaro",explanation:"Mount Kilimanjaro in Tanzania, Africa, is a volcanic mountain \u2014 it is Africa's highest peak. Mount Fuji (Japan) is another famous volcanic mountain. India's only active volcano is Barren Island in the Andaman & Nicobar Islands. The Aravallis are fold mountains and the Vindhyas are block mountains."},{id:"mcq07",difficulty:"Easy",question:"The Deccan Plateau covers which of the following Indian states?",options:["A) Punjab, Haryana, and Uttar Pradesh","B) Karnataka, Maharashtra, and Telangana","C) Rajasthan, Gujarat, and Madhya Pradesh","D) West Bengal, Bihar, and Assam"],correct_answer:"B) Karnataka, Maharashtra, and Telangana",explanation:"The Deccan Plateau covers the states of Karnataka, Maharashtra, Telangana, Andhra Pradesh, and parts of Tamil Nadu and Madhya Pradesh. It forms the large triangular heartland of peninsular India. Bengaluru in Karnataka is located on the Deccan Plateau at about 900 m elevation."},{id:"mcq08",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Why are plains formed by rivers called 'alluvial plains'?",options:["A) Because they are formed by the folding of tectonic plates","B) Because they are located near the sea","C) Because they are formed by sediment (silt and soil) deposited by rivers over time","D) Because they are always perfectly flat and without any features"],correct_answer:"C) Because they are formed by sediment (silt and soil) deposited by rivers over time",explanation:"Alluvial plains are formed when rivers carry small particles of rock, silt, and organic matter from mountains and deposit them on flat land over thousands of years. The deposited material (alluvium) forms rich, fertile soil ideal for agriculture. The Indo-Gangetic Plain is one of the world's most extensive alluvial plains."},{id:"mcq09",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Mount Everest is located on the border of which two countries?",options:["A) India and Nepal","B) Nepal and China","C) China and Bhutan","D) India and China"],correct_answer:"B) Nepal and China",explanation:"Mount Everest is located on the border of Nepal and China (Tibet). In Nepal it is called 'Sagarmatha' and in Tibet it is called 'Chomolungma'. It was first scaled on 29 May 1953 by Sir Edmund Hillary (New Zealand) and Tenzing Norgay Sherpa (Nepal)."},{id:"mcq10",difficulty:"Easy",question:"The Indo-Gangetic Plain is called the 'food bowl' of India because:",options:["A) It has the most number of restaurants in India","B) It produces the largest quantities of food crops due to fertile alluvial soil","C) It is located in the centre of India where most people cook","D) Its soil is infertile but the climate is good for food storage"],correct_answer:"B) It produces the largest quantities of food crops due to fertile alluvial soil",explanation:"The Indo-Gangetic Plain is called the 'food bowl' of India because its deep, fertile alluvial soil (deposited by the Indus, Ganga, and Brahmaputra rivers) supports massive production of wheat, rice, sugarcane, and other crops. It is the most agriculturally productive region in India."},{id:"mcq11",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"How are block mountains different from fold mountains?",options:["A) Block mountains are higher than fold mountains","B) Block mountains are formed by volcanic activity, fold mountains by erosion","C) Block mountains are formed by faults in the crust (blocks lifted/sunk); fold mountains are formed by folding of rocks due to tectonic pressure","D) Block mountains have no flat top; fold mountains always have flat tops"],correct_answer:"C) Block mountains are formed by faults in the crust (blocks lifted/sunk); fold mountains are formed by folding of rocks due to tectonic pressure",explanation:"Block mountains form when the Earth's crust breaks along faults, and sections are lifted or dropped. They have steep cliff-like sides and flat tops (e.g., Vindhya Range). Fold mountains form when tectonic plates collide, causing rocks to fold and buckle upward \u2014 they are more ridge-like and jagged (e.g., Himalayas, Alps)."},{id:"mcq12",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"The Western Ghats are significant for Karnataka mainly because they:",options:["A) Form Karnataka's northern border","B) Intercept monsoon winds and are the source of Karnataka's major rivers","C) Are the site of major coal and iron mining","D) Are home to the largest population of tigers in Asia"],correct_answer:"B) Intercept monsoon winds and are the source of Karnataka's major rivers",explanation:"The Western Ghats intercept the southwest monsoon winds coming from the Arabian Sea, causing heavy rainfall on the western slopes. They are the source of Karnataka's major rivers including the Cauvery, Tungabhadra, and Sharavathi. The Western Ghats are also a UNESCO World Heritage Site and a global biodiversity hotspot."},{id:"mcq13",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which of the following correctly matches the mountain range with its type?",options:["A) Himalayas \u2014 Volcanic; Vindhyas \u2014 Fold; Mount Fuji \u2014 Block","B) Himalayas \u2014 Fold; Vindhyas \u2014 Block; Mount Kilimanjaro \u2014 Volcanic","C) Himalayas \u2014 Block; Alps \u2014 Volcanic; Aravalli \u2014 Fold","D) Himalayas \u2014 Residual; Vindhyas \u2014 Volcanic; Aravalli \u2014 Block"],correct_answer:"B) Himalayas \u2014 Fold; Vindhyas \u2014 Block; Mount Kilimanjaro \u2014 Volcanic",explanation:"Himalayas: fold mountains (formed by Indian and Eurasian plate collision). Vindhyas: block mountains (formed by faulting of the Earth's crust). Mount Kilimanjaro: volcanic mountain (in Tanzania, Africa). This is the correct classification. Mount Fuji (Japan) is also a volcanic mountain."},{id:"mcq14",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Why does Bengaluru have a milder climate than Mumbai despite being further inland?",options:["A) Bengaluru is closer to the equator, so it gets less direct sunlight","B) Bengaluru is located on the Deccan Plateau at about 900 m altitude, making it cooler","C) Bengaluru receives more monsoon rainfall, which keeps it cool","D) Mumbai is on a plateau and Bengaluru is at sea level"],correct_answer:"B) Bengaluru is located on the Deccan Plateau at about 900 m altitude, making it cooler",explanation:"Bengaluru's mild climate is primarily due to its elevation \u2014 it sits on the Deccan Plateau at about 900 m above sea level. Higher altitude means lower temperatures (temperature decreases about 6.5\xb0C per 1,000 m of altitude). Mumbai is at sea level, making it hotter and more humid. This is why Bengaluru is known as a pleasant, garden city."},{id:"mcq15",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"A glacier carves a ________-shaped valley, while a river carves a ________-shaped valley.",options:["A) V-shaped; U-shaped","B) U-shaped; V-shaped","C) W-shaped; Z-shaped","D) Flat; Circular"],correct_answer:"B) U-shaped; V-shaped",explanation:"Glaciers carve U-shaped valleys because the slow-moving ice erodes the valley floor and sides evenly, creating a broad, flat-bottomed U shape. Rivers carve V-shaped valleys because running water cuts mainly downward, creating a narrow V-shape. This is an important distinction in geography \u2014 the valley shape tells us whether it was carved by a glacier or a river."},{id:"mcq16",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"India's only active volcano is located in which island group?",options:["A) Lakshadweep Islands","B) Andaman & Nicobar Islands","C) Maldives","D) Sri Lanka"],correct_answer:"B) Andaman & Nicobar Islands",explanation:"India's only active volcano is Barren Island, located in the Andaman & Nicobar Islands in the Bay of Bengal. It erupted most recently in 2017. It is a volcanic mountain \u2014 formed by accumulated lava from repeated eruptions over thousands of years. The Andaman & Nicobar Islands are part of the same volcanic arc as Indonesia and the Philippines."},{id:"mcq17",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which of the following is the correct reason why mountains are important for rivers?",options:["A) Mountains block all rainfall so rivers don't form near them","B) Mountains receive heavy rainfall and snowfall, which is the source of rivers that flow down their slopes","C) Mountains are too cold for water, so rivers form only on plains","D) Rivers form inside mountains from underground springs only"],correct_answer:"B) Mountains receive heavy rainfall and snowfall, which is the source of rivers that flow down their slopes",explanation:"Mountains receive heavy rainfall and accumulate snow/ice (glaciers). As rain falls and glaciers melt, water flows downhill, forming rivers. The Himalayas are the source of the Ganga, Indus, and Brahmaputra \u2014 three of Asia's greatest rivers. The Western Ghats in Karnataka are the source of the Cauvery and other peninsular rivers."},{id:"mcq18",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"The Aravalli Range in Rajasthan is significant because it is:",options:["A) India's highest mountain range","B) One of the world's oldest fold mountain ranges","C) A volcanic range in western India","D) A plateau formed by river erosion"],correct_answer:"B) One of the world's oldest fold mountain ranges",explanation:"The Aravalli Range is one of the world's oldest fold mountain systems, estimated to be about 350 million years old. It runs through Rajasthan from Gujarat to Delhi. Though once as high as the Himalayas, erosion over millions of years has reduced the Aravallis to gentle hills \u2014 a testament to the power of long-term weathering and erosion."},{id:"mcq19",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"A student says: 'Plains are the most useful landforms, so mountains and plateaus are unnecessary.' Evaluate this statement.",options:["A) Correct \u2014 plains provide everything humans need, mountains and plateaus serve no purpose","B) Incorrect \u2014 mountains provide river water, climate regulation, and forests, while plateaus provide minerals, and both are essential for the plains to remain fertile","C) Correct \u2014 only the Indo-Gangetic Plain matters for human civilization in India","D) Incorrect \u2014 plateaus are important but mountains are genuinely unnecessary for human life"],correct_answer:"B) Incorrect \u2014 mountains provide river water, climate regulation, and forests, while plateaus provide minerals, and both are essential for the plains to remain fertile",explanation:"Mountains are the source of rivers that make plains fertile \u2014 without Himalayan rivers, the Gangetic Plain would be a desert. Mountains also protect plains from cold polar winds. Plateaus contain valuable minerals (Deccan Plateau has iron ore, manganese, and gold). All three landforms are interdependent \u2014 plains depend on mountains for water and plateaus for minerals. The student's view ignores these essential connections."},{id:"mcq20",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"Mount Fuji (Japan) and Barren Island (India) are both volcanic mountains. What characteristic do they share that proves this classification?",options:["A) Both are located near rivers and are made of alluvial soil","B) Both have flat tops like a plateau and were formed by tectonic plate collision","C) Both are cone-shaped and were formed by accumulation of volcanic material (lava, ash) from eruptions","D) Both are located near the equator and are the highest peaks in their respective countries"],correct_answer:"C) Both are cone-shaped and were formed by accumulation of volcanic material (lava, ash) from eruptions",explanation:"Volcanic mountains are characterised by their cone shape (formed as lava and ash pile up around a volcanic vent during eruptions) and their volcanic origin. Mount Fuji is a stratovolcano (layered lava and ash) \u2014 a classic cone. Barren Island has a caldera (collapsed summit crater) typical of volcanoes. Their formation through volcanic activity \u2014 not tectonic folding or faulting \u2014 is what defines them as volcanic mountains."}]}},2744,[]);